Fortinet published CVE-2026-35616 on 2026-04-04, an improper-access-control flaw that bypasses API authentication and authorization in FortiClient EMS 7.4.5 and 7.4.6, marked it known exploited, and fixed it in 7.4.7 and in hotfixes for both affected builds; 7.2 and 8.0 are not affected ([Fortinet PSIRT, 2026-04-04](https://fortiguard.fortinet.com/psirt/FG-IR-26-099)). CISA added it to its Known Exploited Vulnerabilities catalog on 2026-04-06 ([CISA KEV catalog, 2026-04-06](https://www.cisa.gov/sites/default/files/feeds/known_exploited_vulnerabilities.json)). Bishop Fox traced the cause to EMS's Django middleware accepting client-certificate information both from the trusted values Apache's TLS module sets and from client-supplied HTTP headers, which Apache was not configured to strip, so a request can claim a verified client certificate it never presented ([Bishop Fox, 2026-04-07](https://bishopfox.com/blog/api-authentication-bypass-in-forticlient-ems-7-4-5-7-4-6-cve-2026-35616)). Arctic Wolf reported on 2026-05-27 that attackers used the flaw to edit an EMS remote-access profile and insert a script that runs when a managed FortiClient establishes its VPN tunnel: `fortitray.exe` or `ipsec.exe` started `cmd.exe`, which ran an encoded PowerShell command that fetched `FortiEndpoint_Patch.exe`, the EKZ infostealer, which steals Chromium and Gecko browser credentials and cookies and sends them out over HTTP ([Arctic Wolf, 2026-05-27](https://arcticwolf.com/resources/blog/forticlient-ems-exploited-via-cve-2026-35616-to-deliver-ekz-infostealer-disguised-as-a-fortinet-patch/)). Arctic Wolf notes that every managed endpoint became a potential execution target without a separate intrusion into each device ([Arctic Wolf, 2026-05-27](https://arcticwolf.com/resources/blog/forticlient-ems-exploited-via-cve-2026-35616-to-deliver-ekz-infostealer-disguised-as-a-fortinet-patch/)).

**Exposure:** FortiClient EMS 7.4.5 or 7.4.6 without the hotfix or the 7.4.7 upgrade, and every endpoint such a server manages ([Fortinet PSIRT, 2026-04-04](https://fortiguard.fortinet.com/psirt/FG-IR-26-099)).

**Detection:** on endpoints, `fortitray.exe` or `ipsec.exe` spawning `cmd.exe` and then PowerShell with an encoded command at VPN connect, and a new executable named as a Fortinet patch; on the EMS server, remote-access profile or endpoint-policy changes that add an on-connect script ([Arctic Wolf, 2026-05-27](https://arcticwolf.com/resources/blog/forticlient-ems-exploited-via-cve-2026-35616-to-deliver-ekz-infostealer-disguised-as-a-fortinet-patch/)).

**Triage:** an on-connect script an administrator deployed through change control is expected; one that appeared without a matching change, or a child process chain from the VPN client to PowerShell, is the signal.

**Defender takeaway:** on an EMS server that ran 7.4.5 or 7.4.6 unpatched, review every remote-access profile and endpoint policy for scripts nobody approved, and treat browser credentials and session cookies on endpoints that connected through it as exposed.
